<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teresa Teng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1953">1953</a>–<a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Teng Li-Chun, Dèng Lìjūn, Teng Li-yun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Teresa Teng was a Taiwanese singer, born in Yunlin County in 1953 to parents from the mainland, who is credited as a pioneer of modern Chinese popular music. She recorded more than 1,700 songs in Mandarin, Hokkien, Cantonese, Japanese and other languages, and built a second career in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1974. From the late 1970s her songs, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-moon-represents-my-heart">The Moon Represents My Heart</a>, spread through mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> on cassettes; banned there for some years in the early 1980s as bourgeois, they circulated on the black market until the restrictions ended. She never performed on the mainland, supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a> student protests of 1989, and died in Chiang Mai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, in 1995.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teresa-teng.jpg" alt="Teresa Teng" width="330" height="270" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teresa_Teng_US.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Teresa Teng was the only daughter among five children of waishengren parents who had fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> after the communist takeover of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1949. Her father was a soldier in the Republic of China Armed Forces, and the family lived in military dependents' villages, first in Yunlin and then in Pingtung. Her father retired in 1957 and sold cakes to make ends meet. He loved Peking opera and her mother Huangmei opera, and both took her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a>. At six she began voice lessons through an acquaintance of her father who instructed an Air Force band, and her first mentor introduced her to singing before military audiences, a practice she kept up throughout her life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Her professional career began in 1967, when she hosted the television programme One Star a Day. At fourteen she left school to focus on music and signed with Yeu Jow Records, releasing albums of dance tunes, Western covers and folk tunes. She sang in nightclubs and set a record at Paris Night in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taipei">Taipei</a> with 70 consecutive days of 90-minute shows. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> she worked with Polydor from 1975, and her album Island Love Songs: Goodbye My Love won the Ten-Star Prize. Her first Cantonese album, in 1980, was the year's best-seller.</p><p>She entered the Japanese market in 1973. Her first Japanese single, released on 1 March 1974, sold about 30,000 copies, but her second, Airport, released on 1 July 1974, sold 700,000. In 1979 she was caught entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> with a fake Indonesian passport and barred for a year. In 1983 she signed with Taurus Records and released Light Exquisite Feeling, which set twelve Tang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> poems to music and was her first album of entirely new songs. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> her songs were also used in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a>'s psychological war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>, and were blasted from speakers on Kinmen Island towards the mainland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Teng died on 8 May 1995, at the age of 42, while on holiday in Chiang Mai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. Several sources reported a severe asthma attack, while Thai doctors attributed her death to heart failure. No autopsy was performed, because the families of Teng and of her fiancé Paul Quilery declined it, and the police closed the case for lack of evidence.</p><p>She is credited as the first pop superstar of the Far East and a pioneer of modern Chinese popular music. Her songs were recorded in Mandarin, Hokkien, Cantonese, Shanghainese, Japanese, Indonesian, English and Italian. In 2007 she was inducted into the Popular Music Hall of Fame at the Koga Masao Museum of Music in Tokyo, the only non-Japanese person so honoured.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Teresa Teng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The song became famous through <b>Teresa Teng</b>, who recorded it for her Mandarin album Love Songs of the Island, Vol.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-moon-represents-my-heart">The Moon Represents My Heart</a></li><li><blockquote>The Taiwanese singer <b>Teresa Teng</b> recorded an Indonesian-language cover of &quot;Dayung Sampan&quot; while touring Southeast Asia in 1978.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tian-mi-mi">Tian Mi Mi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Baojhong Township, in what is now <a href="/countries/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. Died in Chiang Mai, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=23.72,120.3&amp;year=1980" aria-label="Baojhong Township on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7756 1289 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Teresa Teng"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8206 1559h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8206 1559h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=23.72,120.3&amp;year=1980">What was here in 1980? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
